clearly identifies the information needed to decide to proceed with the therapeutic procedure.\nThis process includes verification of the treatment port(s) and proper treatment positioning. All treatment ports were photographed within electronic medical records. The patient's lead blocking along with gross tumor volume and margin was confirmed. Considering superficial radiotherapy is clinical in setup, this requires the physician and radiation therapist to clarify the location interest being treated against initial images, ultrasound, pathology, and patient anatomy. Care was taken to ensure fields treated were geometrically accurate and properly positioned using therapeutic radiology simulation-aided field setting verification per fraction. This process is also utilized to determine if any prescription or setup changes are necessary. These steps are therefore medically necessary to ensure safe and effective administration of radiation. Ongoing therapeutic radiology simulation-aided field setting verification is ordered throughout the course of therapy.\nA high-frequency ultrasound image was acquired today for a two-dimensional evaluation of the tumor volume, depth, width, breadth, review of prior response to treatment, provide geometric accuracy of field placement, and determine whether to proceed with therapeutic delivery.\nThe field placement and ultrasound imaging, per fraction, is separate and distinct from the initial simulation and is an important task in providing safe administration of superficial radiation therapy. Physician evaluation of the ultrasound delivery will be ongoing throughout the course of treatment and is deemed medically necessary to ensure the efficacy of treatment, whether to proceed with therapeutic delivery, and determine any necessary changes. Today's images were evaluated for determination of continuation of treatment with the current plan or with necessary changes as appropriate. According to the review of verification therapeutic radiology simulation-aided field setting and imaging, no change is required. Additionally, the use of ultrasound visualization and targeted assessment allows the patient to be able to see their cancer progress, encouraging the patient to complete and maintain compliance through the full course of prescribed radiotherapy.\nPer Dr. Rao, continued ultrasound guidance and therapeutic radiology simulation-aided field setting verification per fraction is required for field placement, measurement of tumor depth, tissues evaluation, progress, acute effect monitoring, and determination for therapeutic treatment delivery is appropriate.\nTreatment administered by Caterina Chiaro under the supervision of Dr. Rao.\xa0 \xa0\nUltrasound Evaluation - Ultrasound Guidance (US)\nQuantity: 1\nHigh frequency ultrasound depth(mm): 1.50\nDifference from previous imaging(mm): 0.12\nPlan: Core\nField: 01\nSite: LT FOREHEAD\nPathology: BCC\nLesion Size (cm): 0.7X0.6\nMargin (cm):0.5\nApplicator (cm): 2.0\nShield (cm):1.7\nPrescription #:01\nEnergy (kV):100\nTx time (min):0.46\nTDF:102\nDaily dose (cGy):282.44\nTotal Energy (cGy):5648.80\nNumber of fractions:20\nTx’s per week: 3\nImage Guided Superficial Radiotherapy Treatment Delivery Plan\nField:01\nSite: LT FOREHEAD\nPathology: BCC\nFraction number: 02\nEnergy (kV): 100\nTx time (min): 0.46\nDaily dose (cGy):282.44\nCumulative dose (cGy): 564.88\nComments: no questions or concerns today\xa0\nSurface Radiation (SRT SurfRa)\nLocation: LT Forehead\nQuantity: 1"}], 'has_procedure': True, 'closure_aggregated': []}
