2026-05-29 14:35:43.955 | INFO     | services.clinical_parser:extract_closure_sections:276 - 📊 FINAL CLOSURES: [{'type': 'intermediate', 'size': 2.4, 'location': 'Right Cheek', 'location_group': 'high_risk', 'group_key': 'intermediate_high_risk', 'text': 'C. Excision Benign Lesion & Margins\nClinical Diagnosis: Excised Nevus Dysplastic\nLocation: Right Cheek\nMethod:\xa0 Excision\nLesion Size:\xa01.5 - 1.25\xa0\nThe patient was evaluated preoperative and noted to be in stable condition. \n Informed consent was obtained from the patient. All risks, benefits, and alternatives regarding the survey have been reviewed in detail with the patient. \n This includes risks of bleeding, infection, scarring recurrence of lesion and need for further procedures. \nAfter consent was obtained, attention was turned toward the area of interest which was disinfected and cleaned with alcohol. Local anesthetic, 2% lidocaine with epinephrine was infiltrated around the site. An elliptical incision with the appropriate margins was made using a Number 15 to obtain a full thickness specimen. Dissection of the surrounding/overlying tissue was performed to isolate, expose and remove the lesion. The specimen was placed in a bottle labeled C. #WC23-744. \nRepair: Layered Closure \nThe wound was closed with an intermediate layered closure on 08-02-2023. The wound size with margins is 1.5 - 1.25 cm. The wound was approximated utilizing Using 3-O Nylon sutures. The final closure size is 2.4 cm. \nPressure was applied to achieve hemostasis. Bacitracin was applied at the wound site and it was bandaged. Patient tolerated the procedure well and wound care instructions were reviewed and given to the patient.\nD. Excision Malignant Lesion & Margins\nClinical Diagnosis: Excised Lentigo Malignant\nLocation: Left Neck\nMethod: Excision\nLesion Size: 1.5 - 1.4 cm\nWound Size, including margins: 4.5 x 3 cm\nThe patient was evaluated preoperative and noted to be in stable condition. \n Informed consent was obtained from the patient. All risks, benefits, and alternatives regarding the survey have been reviewed in detail with the patient. \n This includes risks of bleeding, infection, scarring recurrence of lesion and need for further procedures. \nAfter consent was obtained, attention was turned toward the area of interest which was disinfected and cleaned with alcohol. Local anesthetic, 2% lidocaine with epinephrine was infiltrated around the site. An elliptical incision with the appropriate margins was made using a Number 15 to obtain a full thickness specimen. Dissection of the surrounding/overlying tissue was performed to isolate, expose and remove the lesion. The specimen was placed in a bottle labeled D. #WC23-745. \nRepair: Complex Closure \nThe wound was closed with a complex closure on 08-02-2023. The wound size with margins is 4.5 - 3 cm. The wound was approximated utilizing 3-O Vicryl and Using 3-O Nylon sutures. The final closure size is 5.25 cm. \nPressure was applied to achieve hemostasis. Bacitracin was applied at the wound site and it was bandaged. Patient tolerated the procedure well and wound care instructions were reviewed and given to the patient.'}, {'type': 'complex', 'size': 5.25, 'location': 'Left Neck', 'location_group': 'high_risk', 'group_key': 'complex_high_risk', 'text': 'C. Excision Benign Lesion & Margins\nClinical Diagnosis: Excised Nevus Dysplastic\nLocation: Right Cheek\nMethod:\xa0 Excision\nLesion Size:\xa01.5 - 1.25\xa0\nThe patient was evaluated preoperative and noted to be in stable condition. \n Informed consent was obtained from the patient. All risks, benefits, and alternatives regarding the survey have been reviewed in detail with the patient. \n This includes risks of bleeding, infection, scarring recurrence of lesion and need for further procedures. \nAfter consent was obtained, attention was turned toward the area of interest which was disinfected and cleaned with alcohol. Local anesthetic, 2% lidocaine with epinephrine was infiltrated around the site. An elliptical incision with the appropriate margins was made using a Number 15 to obtain a full thickness specimen. Dissection of the surrounding/overlying tissue was performed to isolate, expose and remove the lesion. The specimen was placed in a bottle labeled C. #WC23-744. \nRepair: Layered Closure \nThe wound was closed with an intermediate layered closure on 08-02-2023. The wound size with margins is 1.5 - 1.25 cm. The wound was approximated utilizing Using 3-O Nylon sutures. The final closure size is 2.4 cm. \nPressure was applied to achieve hemostasis. Bacitracin was applied at the wound site and it was bandaged. Patient tolerated the procedure well and wound care instructions were reviewed and given to the patient.\nD. Excision Malignant Lesion & Margins\nClinical Diagnosis: Excised Lentigo Malignant\nLocation: Left Neck\nMethod: Excision\nLesion Size: 1.5 - 1.4 cm\nWound Size, including margins: 4.5 x 3 cm\nThe patient was evaluated preoperative and noted to be in stable condition. \n Informed consent was obtained from the patient. All risks, benefits, and alternatives regarding the survey have been reviewed in detail with the patient. \n This includes risks of bleeding, infection, scarring recurrence of lesion and need for further procedures. \nAfter consent was obtained, attention was turned toward the area of interest which was disinfected and cleaned with alcohol. Local anesthetic, 2% lidocaine with epinephrine was infiltrated around the site. An elliptical incision with the appropriate margins was made using a Number 15 to obtain a full thickness specimen. Dissection of the surrounding/overlying tissue was performed to isolate, expose and remove the lesion. The specimen was placed in a bottle labeled D. #WC23-745. \nRepair: Complex Closure \nThe wound was closed with a complex closure on 08-02-2023. The wound size with margins is 4.5 - 3 cm. The wound was approximated utilizing 3-O Vicryl and Using 3-O Nylon sutures. The final closure size is 5.25 cm. \nPressure was applied to achieve hemostasis. Bacitracin was applied at the wound site and it was bandaged. Patient tolerated the procedure well and wound care instructions were reviewed and given to the patient.'}]
