ue was prepared for the cryostat and sectioned. Each section was coded, cut and stained for microscopic examination. The slides were then analyzed by the Mohs surgeon for any evidence of remaining tumor. Areas noted to be positive on the previous stage (if applicable) were removed with the mohs technique and processed for analysis. This process was repeated until a tumor free plane was obtained. \n1st Stage: 2 Sections, Negative\nPost-Mohs Deficit Size: 1.2 x 1.5 cm.\n Repair Options:\n \n After a discussion of wound management options, \n due to the size and location of the defect, and the need to achieve optimal cosmesis, \n avoid hematoma, the following wound management was agreed upon to provide the best functional and cosmetic result. \n Performed: \n The wound was closed with a Layered Closure on the same day of mohs. \n Reconstruction:\nRepair: Layered Closure\n The wound was closed with an intermediate layered closure on 12-17-2025. The wound involved the dermis and subcutaneous tissue which required a layer repair to minimize tension and promote optimal healing. The closure was performed in two layers; the deep dermis/ subcutaneous layer was approximated utilizing 3-O Vicryl absorbable sutures while the epidermal layer was closed with the use of 3-O Nylon sutures. \nThis layered closure was necessary due to the wounds depth and tissue separation. The location of the wound required reinforcement with a deep dermal closure to prevent breakdown and minimize risk of wound complications. The final closure size is 2.3 cm.\nPressure was applied to achieve hemostasis. Aquaphor was applied at the wound site and it was bandaged. Patient tolerated the procedure well and wound care instructions were reviewed and given to the patient.\nNOTE: null \n Post-Operative:\nThe patient tolerated the procedure well with no complications, and minimal bleeding. The wound was covered with Vaseline and Press Dressing. Detailed oral and written post-operative instructions were reviewed and given to the patient. The patient instructed to remove dressing in 24 hours, sleep with the surgical site elevated this evening, avoid alcohol, trauma and tobacco.\n Patient was informed to return in 2 Weeks.'}]
